questions.q:
A village pond has no fish, and a farmer grows mustard, a flowering crop, right beside it. The fisheries officer plans to put fish into the pond so the village can sell fish. The farmer asks how this will affect his mustard. Using the chapter's pond study, explain:
(a) what will happen to the dragonflies near the pond;
(b) what will happen to the bees, flies and butterflies;
(c) what will happen to the seeds of his mustard;
(d) whether the farmer should welcome the plan, and what could happen to his crop if the pond were later overfished.

'An ecosystem is more than the community living in it.' Justify this statement, using a lake as your example, in five parts:
(a) what the community of the lake is, with two members;
(b) two abiotic components of the lake;
(c) one way the lake's organisms depend on its abiotic components;
(d) one way the lake's organisms change its abiotic components;
(e) how the levels individual, population, community and ecosystem fit together.

Soapy water from washing clothes starts draining into a village pond, and within a few weeks many of its water plants die (illustrative). Using the chapter's idea that one change leads to another:
(a) explain what happens to the oxygen in the water;
(b) explain what happens to the fish;
(c) explain why the insects increase;
(d) explain what the farmers nearby may be forced to do, and its further effect;
(e) explain why such an effect is called a cascading effect.

'The Green Revolution solved one problem but created others.' Evaluate this statement in five parts:
(a) the problem India faced between 1950 and 1965, and its cause;
(b) what the Green Revolution used to solve it;
(c) one way the result helped India;
(d) three reasons these methods are now considered unsustainable;
(e) two practices from the chapter that make farming more sustainable, and your overall judgement of the statement.
